Build succeeds. Here's the complete solution:

---

## Program.cs (stdio transport)

```csharp
var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);
builder.Logging.AddConsole(o => o.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient("api", client => {
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();   // auto-discovers [McpServerToolType] classes

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

## Tool class (`Tools/ApiTools.cs`)

Uses **constructor injection** — `IHttpClientFactory` and `ILogger<T>` are resolved by DI and never appear in the LLM-visible tool schema.

Four tools are exposed: `GetPost`, `ListPosts`, `GetComments`, `CreatePost` — each with `[Description]` on the method and every parameter so LLMs know when/how to call them.

## How DI works for MCP tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

1. **Constructor injection** (non-static class, used here) — DI services are passed via the primary constructor. The class is instantiated per-call by the DI container.

2. **Method parameter injection** (static class) — DI services appear as method parameters alongside user-facing params. The SDK distinguishes them: registered services are injected silently, everything else becomes part of the tool schema.

**Key rule:** `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>`, and any DI-registered service are **automatically injected** and hidden from the schema. Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed to the LLM.

The project is at `ApiMcpServer/` and builds cleanly.
